# 1. Introduction

**Draft.** Numbers come from the files named in `data/frame-numbers.json` (the final evaluation run,
hls-est `{{run_hlsest_git}}`, comparison generated {{run_generated}}). VITIS_NOTES are cited as N*n*,
OPEN_ISSUES as O*n* and recorded decisions as MEMOS #*n*; all are in `hls-doc/notes/` and are the
appendix references of this thesis.

## 1.1 The problem: the decisions of an HLS tool are closed

High-level synthesis lets a designer write an FPGA accelerator in C or C++ and steer it with pragmas
(`pipeline`, `unroll`, `array_partition`, `bind_storage`, `dataflow`, `interface`). Surveys find that HLS
gives up some quality of results against hand-written RTL but saves a large share of development time
[@lahti2019; @cong2022fpga]. The price is that much of the hardware is decided by the tool, not by the
designer:
- whether a loop the designer did not annotate is pipelined, unrolled or flattened;
- whether an array that is indexed by constants is split into registers;
- how many copies of a memory are built, so that a pipelined loop can read it several times per cycle;
- whether a small array goes to LUT RAM or block RAM;
- and which aspect ratio each block RAM uses.

AMD Vitis HLS [@amd2025ug1399] documents its pragmas and some of its defaults, but not the rules behind
these automatic decisions. Its front end is open source [@xilinx_hlsllvm]. The optimisation passes after
it, the scheduler, the binder and the RTL generator are not.

A designer therefore learns the cost of a design only after running synthesis, which takes from seconds to
hours per design. The median C synthesis of the catalogue used here takes {{ev_time_vitis_median_s}} s,
and {{vitis_timeout}} designs did not finish within the harness's time limit. Design-space exploration,
early feasibility checks and teaching all suffer from this. So does every research tool that wants to
reason about what Vitis *will* do with a source file.

**Research questions.** The thesis asks three questions:

- **RQ1 (inference).** Can the automatic memory decisions of a closed HLS tool be inferred from the
  outside, without disassembling it, as explicit rules that each rest on reproducible experiments?
- **RQ2 (fidelity).** How closely does an open implementation of those rules reproduce the tool's BRAM
  allocation, per design and per memory, on a large catalogue of real designs, and what does it get
  wrong?
- **RQ3 (use).** Are the inferred decisions complete enough to serve as more than an estimate, for
  example to drive code generation?

## 1.2 Why block RAM, and why per memory

This thesis concentrates on one resource, the 18 Kbit block RAM (BRAM_18K) of AMD 7-series devices
[@amd_ug473], for three reasons.

- **It is often the binding resource.** On the small Zynq-7000 part used here ({{vitis_parts}}, with
  {{ev_device_capacity}} BRAM_18K), BRAM has the highest utilisation of the four resource classes (BRAM,
  DSP, FF, LUT) in {{util_bram_highest}} of the {{util_designs}} designs with a Vitis utilisation report.
  For {{ev_over_device_vitis}} of the {{ev_vitis_results}} designs with a Vitis result, Vitis allocates
  more BRAM_18K than the part has.
- **It is decided by many interacting rules.** The BRAM of one array depends on:
  - the front end: dead-array removal, constant folding, struct splitting;
  - the loop decisions: pipelining, unrolling, flattening;
  - partitioning: user pragmas and the tool's automatic partitions;
  - the schedule: how many accesses fall into one cycle;
  - the storage choice: LUT RAM below a size threshold (N5);
  - the replication of read-heavy memories (N4, N38);
  - and the mapping of a bank onto RAMB18 aspect modes (N6, N8).

  Getting the BRAM of one array right means getting each of these decisions right for that array.
- **It is countable exactly.** Vitis reports BRAM_18K per memory in its synthesis report, so a prediction
  is either equal or not. Where the two tools name a memory alike, the claim is falsifiable per array.

Matching only design totals is not enough, because totals can match by cancellation: an over-count in one
array hides an under-count in another. This happened during the project, for example on PolyBench
`atax`, `bicg` and `mvt` before sequential dual-port use was modelled (O3). The harness therefore also
joins hls-est and Vitis per memory, by the C variable name. A change was kept only if every design it
changed was explained per array (the project's adoption rule, Section 3.5). The per-array join has a
limit, measured in Chapter 6: only {{ev_pa_aligned_pct}} % of the BRAM-carrying array rows align by name,
and {{ev_pa_non_array_pct}} % of Vitis's BRAM lies outside every memory table. Accuracy is therefore
reported per design, and per array only where the join can score it.

## 1.3 Approach

The approach is behavioural black-box inference, in the spirit of measuring closed processors with
micro-benchmarks [@abel2019uops] and of model learning for black-box systems [@vaandrager2017model]:

1. Run Vitis HLS {{vitis_versions}} on a large catalogue of real designs (the HLSFactory catalogue
   [@abikaram2024hlsfactory], {{designs}} designs in {{datasets}} datasets). For each design, record the
   memory and loop tables, the transformation log and the intermediate LLVM IR files Vitis writes.
2. Where a prediction differs from Vitis, find the first stage where Vitis and hls-est diverge. Prove the
   behaviour with a minimal kernel, often a sweep over one parameter across a suspected threshold,
   synthesised with the same configuration.
3. Write the rule down as a numbered note with its kernels and evidence paths, and mark anything not
   proven as unconfirmed. Implement it as a general mechanism, never as a branch on a benchmark, an array
   name or a size.
4. Re-run the whole catalogue. Keep the change only if no exact design is lost without a per-array
   explanation.

The method uses only:
- what Vitis documents or writes as output, and its own `-help` text;
- the open-source front end;
- for device facts, a read-only look at the installed device database (MEMOS #42).

Closed binaries are never disassembled or decompiled (Chapter 8 discusses this choice).

Because step 2 starts from catalogue mismatches, the catalogue is both the development set and the
evaluation set. Every accuracy number in this thesis is an in-sample number (Section 6.8).

## 1.4 Contributions

The contributions answer the three research questions.

**RQ1, inference.**

1. **A catalogue of Vitis HLS decision rules inferred by black-box experiments.** {{n_notes}} numbered
   notes, N1 to N{{n_last}}. {{n_notes_confirmed}} are confirmed: {{n_notes_confirmed_kernels}} by
   standalone kernels and {{n_notes_confirmed_corpus}} only on catalogue designs or ground-truth reports.
   {{n_notes_partly}} are partly confirmed, {{n_notes_unconfirmed}} unconfirmed, and {{n_notes_method}}
   are notes on measurement method. Each note names its kernels and run directories. Examples:
   - the automatic pipeline / unroll / flatten decision (N3, {{N3.kernels}} kernels);
   - RAM_1WNR replication (N4, {{N4.kernels}} kernels; N38);
   - the BRAM count per bank from RAMB18 aspect modes (N6, {{N6.kernels}} kernels) and its
     reconciliation with the open-source resource formula (N8);
   - the partitions that remain with throughput-driven partitioning off (N7, {{N7.kernels}} kernels);
   - Vitis' automatic inlining rule (N18, {{N18.kernels}} kernels);
   - the dataflow ping-pong buffers (N33, {{N33.kernels}} kernels).

**RQ2, fidelity.**

2. **hls-est, an open implementation of those rules in MLIR/CIRCT** [@lattner2021mlir; @circt]. It is a
   {{pipeline_passes}}-pass pipeline, {{pipeline_own_passes}} of whose passes were written or modified for
   this work. The pipeline mirrors Vitis' stages: front-end deletion and folding, loop decisions,
   partitioning and width narrowing, modulo scheduling, and storage and BRAM pricing. It explains every
   number in a machine-readable debug report. That the report never changes an estimate is tested, not
   proven (Section 3.7).
3. **A validation infrastructure.** It has four parts:
   - {{vitis_ok}} successful Vitis syntheses, with {{vitis_memory_rows}} memory-table rows and
     {{vitis_loop_rows}} loop-table rows;
   - a harness that joins hls-est and Vitis per design and, where names align, per array;
   - a regression net of lit tests that pins the output of every catalogue design: {{mirror_tests}}
     generated Mirror tests and {{lit_passes}} hand-written pass tests;
   - a C/C++ front end that compiles the real Vitis headers. The Polygeist front end cgeist
     [@moses2021polygeist] was extended to understand the Xilinx front-end extensions and to copy Vitis'
     front-end behaviours. Its output is checked by differential execution against native builds:
     {{sem_equal}} of the {{sem_checked}} designs that could be checked are bit-exact.
4. **An evaluation on the full catalogue (in-sample).** Counting each distinct source once and leaving out
   zero-BRAM designs, {{ev_distinct_nonzero_exact}} of {{ev_distinct_nonzero_compared}} designs are exact
   and {{ev_distinct_nonzero_in_band}} are exact or over by at most 15 %. Over all {{compared}} compared
   designs, the figures are {{exact}} ({{exact_pct}} %) and {{in_goal}} ({{in_goal_pct}} %). A naive
   declared-size baseline is exact on {{ev_b0_exact}}. Every remaining out-of-band design is attributed per
   array to a cause class (Chapter 6).

**RQ3, use.**

5. **An RTL back end as an application** (Chapter 7, on a separate branch and a different hls-est commit,
   `{{rtl_hlsest}}`). hls-est's static decisions are lowered to Verilog with Vitis' block-level protocol
   and checked by co-simulation against each design's own C testbench. {{rtl_verilog}} of the
   {{rtl_catalogue}} catalogue designs ({{rtl_verilog_pct}} %) get Verilog and {{rtl_cosim_pass}} pass,
   with {{rtl_wrong_code}} wrong-code mismatches. Only {{rtl_selfcheck}} of the passing testbenches notice
   inverted outputs.

**Engineering.** {{config_keys}} configuration keys and {{plugin_interfaces}} plugin interfaces separate
the AMD device and the Vitis policies from the analysis. They cover storage selection, memory
architecture, memory mapping, pipelining and partition policies, fold rules and the scheduling
interfaces. The worked retargeting to Microchip PolarFire is not validated against any tool, so this is a
software-engineering contribution, not a validated result (Chapter 5).

## 1.5 Scope

The ground truth is one tool version, one device and one clock ({{vitis_versions}}, {{vitis_parts}},
{{vitis_clocks}}), with `syn.array_partition.throughput_driven=off`, the configuration of the original
project. Only BRAM is modelled. DSP, FF and LUT estimation (Phase 6) depend on Vitis' schedule and binding
and are future work. The success criterion was fixed at the start of the project: for every design with
ground truth, hls-est equals Vitis or over-counts by at most 15 %. An under-count is always out of band,
because an estimate that promises a design fits when it does not is worse than a pessimistic one.

## 1.6 Authorship and use of AI assistance

**To be written and signed by the author; the facts below are generated from git and must be checked
against the university's rules.**

- **What existed before.** The estimator this work started from is the `Dialect/Resource` code on `main`
  of `~/circt` at the branch point `{{circt_branch_point}}` ({{circt_branch_point_date}}):
  {{files_resource_before}} files, {{loc_resource_before}} lines. At the snapshot `{{circt_head}}`, the
  same directories hold {{files_resource}} files and {{loc_resource}} lines (Section 5.9).
- **AI assistance.** All {{circt_commits}} commits of the hls-est branch and all {{polygeist_commits}}
  commits of the Polygeist branch up to the snapshot carry a co-author trailer naming an AI coding agent
  (Claude, Anthropic): {{circt_commits_coauthored}} and {{polygeist_commits_coauthored}} of them. The
  experiments, the implementation, the notes and the reviews of Section 3.6 were carried out by AI agents
  under the author's direction. The reviews were made by agents of the same model family as the
  implementation, so they are independent of the implementing session, not of the model. This draft
  itself was generated by such an agent from the project's result files.
- **What the author decided.** The project's recorded decisions (MEMOS) name the author's decisions,
  among them the target configuration, the success criterion, the move to the real Vitis headers and the
  scope. The author must state here which parts of the design, analysis and text are his own.

## 1.7 Outline

Chapter 2 introduces Vitis HLS and the related work on HLS estimation, memory partitioning, HLS tool
testing and open HLS compilers. Chapter 3 describes the inference method and Chapter 4 the inferred rules;
the kernel tables of the rules are in Appendix A. Chapter 5 describes the implementation. Chapter 6
evaluates hls-est against Vitis. Chapter 7 presents the RTL back end. Chapter 8 discusses threats to
validity, limitations and future work.
